fix(qbzd): publish playback events on the bus so MPRIS, scrobbling and SSE work

The core never emits CoreEvent::{TrackStarted, PlaybackStateChanged,
PositionUpdated, VolumeChanged}. The desktop does not need it: its Slint poll
loop reads the player every 450 ms and pushes to the UI, the tray and MPRIS by
hand (qbz/src/playback.rs). The daemon has no such loop, and all three of its
playback surfaces subscribe to the adapter bus instead:

  mpris.rs           org.mpris.MediaPlayer2 stays PlaybackStatus="Stopped"
                     with empty Metadata forever; only the one-time boot seed
                     ever lands. Volume reads 0.75 because the seed is the sole
                     update that gets through - the signature of this bug.
  scrobble_engine.rs waits on TrackStarted, so no scrobble is ever sent and no
                     "now playing" reaches Last.fm / ListenBrainz.
  api/sse.rs         GET /api/events (and `qbzd watch`) never report a track
                     change or a play/pause.

There is a fourth, silent consequence: qbz-media-controls holds the logind
sleep:idle inhibitor from linux.rs, driven by the same set_playback updates, so
the daemon never inhibited sleep during playback and the machine could suspend
mid-track.

Add one publisher rather than three pollers. playback_events.rs mirrors the
desktop poll at the driver's 450 ms cadence, diffs against the previous tick and
emits the edges onto the bus every surface already subscribes to. Playback
origin is irrelevant: the CLI, the HTTP API and a QConnect controller all drive
the one core().player().

Notes on the implementation:
- TrackStarted is emitted before PlaybackStateChanged so a subscriber has the
  metadata before the status referring to it; an MPRIS widget otherwise flashes
  the previous track.
- During a gapless hand-off the queue cursor and the id the engine plays
  disagree for a tick, until the driver's SyncCursorTo lands. The publisher
  waits instead of publishing the wrong track, with an 8-tick ceiling so a stuck
  cursor cannot turn this into a permanent per-tick queue read.
- PositionUpdated is whole seconds and gated on Playing, so a playing track
  yields about one event per second, well inside the bus's 256 slots.
- The task holds only a Weak<AppRuntime>, upgraded per tick, and is aborted and
  joined before the subscribers it feeds, so no strong reference is in flight
  when drop(booted) releases the audio device (the #521 ordering).
- It is spawned LAST, after every subscriber. The runtime is multi-threaded and
  the first interval tick completes immediately, so a publisher created earlier
  could emit into a bus nobody has subscribed to yet; broadcast::send drops
  those events and a missed TrackStarted is never re-emitted, since the
  publisher latches the track id it published.

This touches no audio backend and no routing: the publisher is a pure reader of
the player state and never commands it, so it cannot race the playback driver.

Verified with cargo check -p qbzd and cargo test -p qbzd (292 tests pass). The
remaining dead_code warning on api/playback.rs::device_error is pre-existing.

